slftp API & Web UI Quickstart
Note: API/Web UI are early beta; Windows use is currently untested.

Prerequisites
- Configure config/slftp.ini.
- Build backend with Free Pascal (make).
- (Optional) Build Web UI with Node.js/npm (for static assets).

Configure API
Edit config/slftp.ini:
[api]
enabled=1
host=127.0.0.1
port=8089
apikey=<your_long_secret_here>
- Use 127.0.0.1 for local-only; 0.0.0.0 if you intentionally expose it (prefer behind TLS/proxy).
- Set a strong apikey. If set, send Authorization: Bearer <apikey> on API calls.

Build backend
make        # or make slftp_debug

Build Web UI (optional, for browser admin)
cd web-ui
npm install
# If apikey is set in slftp.ini, pass it during build:
VITE_API_TOKEN=<your_apikey_here> npm run build
# Otherwise just:
npm run build

Built assets land in web-ui/dist/. Copy/deploy to web/ folder for runtime:
cp -r web-ui/dist/* web/
At runtime, only the web/ folder is needed; Node/npm is not required.

Windows (PowerShell) example:
cd .\web-ui
npm install
$env:VITE_API_TOKEN="<your_apikey_here>"; npm run build
Copy-Item -Recurse -Force .\dist\* ..\web\

Deploy & Run

Linux/Mac (building and deploying to separate directory):
# Stop running slftp
killall slftp  # or Ctrl+C

# Deploy backend binary
cp slftp /your/slftp/path/

# Deploy Web UI assets (clean old files first to avoid browser cache issues!)
rm -rf /your/slftp/path/web/*
cp -r web-ui/dist/* /your/slftp/path/web/

# Start slftp in production directory
cd /your/slftp/path
./slftp

Windows (PowerShell):
# Stop slftp (Ctrl+C)

# Deploy backend binary
Copy-Item slftp.exe C:\your\slftp\path\

# Deploy Web UI assets (clean old files first!)
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force C:\your\slftp\path\web\*
Copy-Item -Recurse -Force web-ui\dist\* C:\your\slftp\path\web\

# Start slftp
cd C:\your\slftp\path
.\slftp.exe

If building and running in same directory:
./slftp

API base: http://<host>:<port>/api
Web UI: http://<host>:<port>/ (served from web/ if built)

Call the API (curl examples, only implemented endpoints)
- System status:
  curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer <apikey>" \
    http://127.0.0.1:8089/api/ApiSystemService/GetStatus

- Sites list:
  curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer <apikey>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"Filter":""}' \
    http://127.0.0.1:8089/api/ApiSitesService/GetSites

- Queue stats:
  curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer <apikey>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"SiteName":""}' \
    http://127.0.0.1:8089/api/ApiQueueService/GetQueueStats

See API_DOCUMENTATION for the short list of working endpoints; all others are currently stubbed.

Web UI configuration
- The Web UI uses relative path '/api' to reach the API (same host/port)
- Host/port from slftp.ini [api] section apply to both Web UI and API automatically
- API token must be passed during build via VITE_API_TOKEN (it gets embedded in the JS bundle)
- Important: If you change the apikey in slftp.ini, rebuild the Web UI with the new token!
- For dev server: npm run dev (runs on http://localhost:5173)
  Set VITE_API_BASE_URL and VITE_API_TOKEN env vars if connecting to remote API

Updating after code changes

If you changed backend code (Pascal files):
Linux/Mac:
1. Rebuild: make clean && make slftp
2. Stop slftp: killall slftp (or Ctrl+C)
3. Deploy (if different dir): cp slftp /your/slftp/path/
4. Restart: cd /your/slftp/path && ./slftp

Windows:
1. Rebuild: fpc -MDelphi -O3 -Xs slftp.lpr
2. Stop slftp (Ctrl+C)
3. Deploy (if different dir): Copy-Item slftp.exe C:\your\slftp\path\
4. Restart: cd C:\your\slftp\path; .\slftp.exe

If you changed frontend code (TypeScript/React files):
Linux/Mac:
1. Rebuild: cd web-ui && npm run build
2. Deploy: rm -rf /your/slftp/path/web/* && cp -r dist/* /your/slftp/path/web/
3. Hard-refresh browser: Ctrl+Shift+R (no slftp restart needed)

Windows:
1. Rebuild: cd web-ui; npm run build
2. Deploy: Remove-Item -Recurse -Force C:\your\slftp\path\web\*; Copy-Item -Recurse -Force dist\* C:\your\slftp\path\web\
3. Hard-refresh browser: Ctrl+Shift+R

If you changed API key in config:
1. Restart slftp to load new config
2. Login again in Web UI with new apikey (old token will be invalid)

Security notes
- Keep apikey secret and prefer binding to localhost unless fronted by TLS/proxy.
- API key protection currently only applies to /api/* endpoints.
- Web UI has no authentication yet (TODO) - restrict access via firewall/proxy if needed.
- For production use, consider running behind nginx/traefik with TLS and basic auth.
